      "source_example_id": "e-8543",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function",
      "kp_id": "e-8543"
    },
    "q-163195": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163195",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/1bed15fe8f829f2a2248d5f366e4ca5df84274a767add87b3d26da9d3c2ab12d.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $x$-axis and the lines $y=x$ and $y=4-x$, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=2x,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,y\\le x\\le 4-y,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/fee47e2abe427ce056c77adb5df9a003263bade6e6e3e8d62ca789c162e2348c.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{y}^{4-y}2x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{x}^{2}{y}^{2}]_{x=y}^{x=4-y}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}(4-y)^{2}-{y}^{4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}16{y}^{2}-8{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{3}-2{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{128}{3}-32 \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
