      "source_example_id": "e-8543",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Moment of Inertia of a Lamina Given a Mass Density Function",
      "kp_id": "e-8543"
    },
    "q-163175": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163175",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/eb4ea276f4b876ce42b3701d60c1a5967769b62b328c16e9f9e75ef050f18d6f.png)\n\nA plate occupies the region $D$ enclosed by the $y$-axis and the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=x+2$ in the first quadrant, as shown above. If the mass density function of $D$ is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=15x,$ find the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/f11f3026c9cc7f315781470791f03d3df97d7f292f8b713a1903f021bd5bf6c6.png)\n\nUsing the formula for ${I}_{y},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}{x}^{2}(15x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x+2}15{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{3}[y]_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=x+2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{3}(x+2-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}15{x}^{4}+30{x}^{3}-15{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[3{x}^{5}+\\frac{15}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =96+120-160 \\\\  & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
