      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/7545f5c48a86aa8ab7a8f46272e01f3021b7d7be10eea2019ce31c3225ca2f4d.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ and uniform mass density occupies the region $D$ enclosed between the $x$-axis and the lines $y=x$ and $y=2-x,$ as shown above. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type II region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/b1ced30e4cc1fe984b56707f11e8720f2ee68c5b204b6ea7b8d607a65276acca.png)\n\nThe area of the triangular plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nSince the plate has uniform density, every unit of area contains the same amount of mass. Therefore, the mass density is constant and is given by the total mass divided by the total area:\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=M.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{2-y}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{y}^{2-y}\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-y)^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[-\\frac{1}{4}(2-y)^{4}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{M}{12}{[(2-y)^{4}+{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{M}{12}(1+1-16-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
