      "extraction_error_parts": {},
      "assets": [],
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/observations/step-q163177-37643d0998a74af78a8a.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/observations/step-q163177-37643d0998a74af78a8a.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14129388/topics/2027/lesson",
      "captured_at": 1791774861.3182256,
      "math_academy_id": "q-163177",
      "topic_id": "2027",
      "sequence_position": null,
      "source_step": {
        "dom_id": "step-q163177",
        "placement_id": null,
        "content_id": null,
        "type": null
      },
      "source_example_id": "e-12482",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Moment of Inertia Given a Description of a Mass Density Function",
      "kp_id": "e-12482"
    },
    "q-163190": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163190",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/e818c7ab51d26b642039c4f5e5157dee97772eab41b130ff8c9243f102c5fa20.png)\n\nA triangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant enclosed by the lines $y=3x,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. The mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/b7457bee1577c81fc5c04d4b1a116e82790d7d251dc61bd0611d00aae7d34bfe.png)\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(3x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =k{[3{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{M}{3}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=\\frac{M}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}(3x)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}{[2{x}^{6}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
