    "q-163176": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163176",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "A rectangular plate of mass $M$ occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$\nThe mass density at each point is proportional to the square of its distance from the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the lamina about the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{3}+{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =k{[\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =k(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $k=\\frac{3M}{2}$ and hence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=k({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=\\frac{3M}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\cdot ({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}{[\\frac{1}{5}y+\\frac{1}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{2}\\cdot \\frac{14}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
