    "q-162988": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162988",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/82ed1d9c8965c445488256f2319bd2e1a25b2f685d5ad39c88f2b2ba7f7f5525.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(81-0)\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
