    "q-163253": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163253",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/74ae29ecab0a2d87fb020cc0801277a4a08dd11d38ea2b81f0718c6d30a19598.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ bounded by the polar circles $r=2$ and $r=3,$ as shown above. The mass density at any point is proportional to its distance from the origin. Find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D,$ in polar coordinates, can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nFrom the description, the mass density function can be written as $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr,$ where $k$ is a constant of proportionality. We can find an expression for $k$ in terms of $M$ by integrating the density function over the plate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}kr\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =k({\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =k\\cdot \\frac{19}{3}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{38\\pi k}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=kr=\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }r.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }({\\int }_{2}^{3}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}\\,{|}_{2}^{3}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3M}{38\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{211}{5}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{633M}{95}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
