    "q-163244": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163244",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14129388/assets/ccf2ad2b3d8b39dbacdcdce6bd317c5720ce3b750249f6c328e60d3c74ddb4ef.png)\n\nConsider a plate of mass $M$ that occupies the region $D$ in the lower half plane bounded by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ shown above. Given that the mass is distributed uniformly across the plate, find the moment of inertia of the plate about the origin.",
      "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda (r,\\theta ),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $x$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the $y$-axis, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n- The moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ={I}_{x}+{I}_{y}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ in polar coordinates can be expressed as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the plate $D$ is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the mass is distributed uniformly, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{2M}{\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }({\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi }^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2M}{\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
